  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "n1",
      "x": 130,
      "y": 60,
      "w": 540,
      "h": 175,
      "text": "AN AND-REGION IS THE INTERSECTION.\n\nThe dashed rule down the middle of Alive is the notation for \"both of these at once\". Body and Arms are not alternatives; the machine is in one state of each, always.\n\nAccepting needs run AND drawn. Run 'move draw' — accept. 'move' alone — reject, because Arms is still holstered. 'draw' alone — reject, because Body is still idle.\n\nThat is not an analogy. On a symbol, EVERY region takes a transition or none does, so the language of an AND-region is literally the intersection of its regions' languages — the product construction that proves regular languages are closed under intersection, drawn as a picture."
    },
    {
      "id": "n2",
      "x": 130,
      "y": 700,
      "w": 540,
      "h": 175,
      "text": "AND THIS IS WHERE SUCCINCTNESS TURNS EXPONENTIAL.\n\nElsewhere a region buys you 1 arrow instead of N. Here two regions of two states denote 2x2 = 4 configurations, and a third would make 8, not 6. The picture grows by addition; the automaton it denotes grows by multiplication. That is the actual theorem about statecharts.\n\nTwo things that bite:\n\n· 'Ignore this event' is a self-loop on each LEAF. A self-loop drawn on the REGION exits and re-enters it, which snaps that region back to its default entry. Every t3–t6, t9–t12 arrow here exists for that reason.\n\n· 'die' is drawn on Alive itself, so it is GLOBAL — one arrow tears down both regions at once, and the flattening has exactly one dead configuration rather than one per surviving pair."
    }
  ],
  "noteN": 2,
  "meta": {
    "title": "Orthogonality: L(A ∥ B) = L(A) ∩ L(B)",
    "blurb": "Body and Arms run concurrently inside one region. Regions fire synchronously, so the language of an AND-region is the intersection of its regions' — and flattening it is the product construction. Two regions of two states denote four configurations; a third makes eight, not six.",
